## Proof

**Setup and reductions.** Since $G$ is finite abelian, decompose $G = G_p \oplus G_{p'}$ into its $p$-primary and $p'$-primary components. These are characteristic, so $\text{Aut}(G) \cong \text{Aut}(G_p) \times \text{Aut}(G_{p'})$, and $H \subseteq G[p] = G_p[p]$. Thus we may assume $G$ is a $p$-group.

Write $G \cong \bigoplus_{i=1}^k \mathbb{Z}_{p^{a_i}}$ with $1 \le a_1 \le \cdots \le a_k$. Then $G[p] \cong \mathbb{F}_p^k$ with basis $\{f_1,\dots,f_k\}$ where $f_i = p^{a_i-1}e_i$.

**Height filtration.** Define the *height* of $g \in G[p]$ as $h(g) = \max\{n : g \in p^n G\}$. One checks that $g = \sum c_i f_i$ satisfies $h(g) = \min\{a_i - 1 : c_i \ne 0\}$. Set $F_n = G[p] \cap p^n G = \text{span}\{f_i : a_i > n\}$, giving a flag $G[p] = F_0 \supset F_1 \supset \cdots \supset F_{a_k - 1} \supset F_{a_k} = 0$.

**Step 1: Image of $\text{Aut}(G) \to \text{GL}(G[p])$ is the parabolic $P = \text{Stab}(\text{flag})$.**

An automorphism $\varphi$ of $G$ sends $e_i \mapsto \sum_j a_{ji} e_j$ where $a_{ji}$ is divisible by $p^{a_j - a_i}$ whenever $a_j > a_i$. The induced matrix on $G[p]$ in the basis $\{f_i\}$ has $(t,s)$-entry $a_{ts} \cdot p^{a_s - a_t} \pmod{p}$:
- If $a_t \ge a_s$: the entry is free (any value in $\mathbb{F}_p$ achievable).
- If $a_t < a_s$: the entry is $0 \pmod{p}$ (forced).

Grouping indices by equal exponents $b_1 < \cdots < b_m$ with blocks $I_1,\dots,I_m$, the image consists of block-*lower*-triangular matrices with arbitrary $\text{GL}(|I_j|,\mathbb{F}_p)$ diagonal blocks and arbitrary below-diagonal blocks — exactly the parabolic subgroup $P$ stabilizing the flag $\{F_n\}$.

**Surjectivity onto $P$:** Each diagonal block lifts via $\text{Aut}(\mathbb{Z}_{p^{b_j}}^{|I_j|}) \twoheadrightarrow \text{GL}(|I_j|,\mathbb{F}_p)$ (reduce mod $p$). Each below-diagonal entry $m_{ts}$ (with $a_t > a_s$) is realized by setting $a_{ts} = m_{ts} \cdot p^{a_t - a_s}$. The resulting matrix is invertible since its mod-$p$ reduction is $M \in P$. Hence $\text{Aut}(G) \twoheadrightarrow P$.

**Step 2: Characterization of extendable automorphisms.**

An automorphism $\alpha \in \text{Aut}(H) \cong \text{GL}(2,\mathbb{F}_p)$ extends to $\text{Aut}(G)$ iff there exists $M \in P$ with $M(H) = H$ and $M|_H = \alpha$.

*Forward direction:* If $M \in P$ preserves $H$, then for every $n$: $\alpha(H \cap F_n) = M(H \cap F_n) = M(H) \cap M(F_n) = H \cap F_n$. Equivalently, $h(\alpha(g)) = h(g)$ for all $g \in H$.

*Backward direction:* Suppose $\alpha(H \cap F_n) = H \cap F_n$ for all $n$. Since $\dim H = 2$, the induced flag on $H$ has one of two forms:

**Case 1: All nonzero elements of $H$ have the same height $n_0$.** Then $H \subseteq F_{n_0}$ and $H \cap F_{n_0+1} = 0$, so $H$ lies in the graded piece $F_{n_0}/F_{n_0+1}$. Choose a flag-adapted basis of $G[p]$ extending any basis $\{h_1,h_2\}$ of $H$. Define $M$ by $M|_H = \alpha$ and $M = \text{id}$ on the complement. Since $H$ sits in a single graded piece, $M$ preserves the full flag, so $M \in P$. By surjectivity, $\alpha$ lifts to $\text{Aut}(G)$.

**Case 2: $H$ has elements of two different heights.** Let $n^* < n^{**}$ be the two height values. Set $L = H \cap F_{n^{**}}$ (1-dimensional, the elements of maximal height). The flag on $H$ is $0 \subset L \subset H$, and $\alpha$ preserves it iff $\alpha(L) = L$.

Choose $h_1 \in L\setminus\{0\}$ (height $n^{**}$) and $h_2 \in H \setminus L$ (height $n^*$). Then $\alpha(h_1) = c_1 h_1$ and $\alpha(h_2) = a h_1 + c_2 h_2$ with $c_1, c_2 \in \mathbb{F}_p^*$. Extend $\{h_1\}$ to a flag-adapted basis of $F_{n^{**}}$, then $\{h_1, h_2\}$ to a flag-adapted basis of $F_{n^*}$, and continue to a full flag-adapted basis of $G[p]$. Define $M$ by $M(h_i) = \alpha(h_i)$ and $M = \text{id}$ on all other basis vectors.

Verification that $M \in P$:
- For $n > n^{**}$: $F_n$ contains neither $h_1$ nor $h_2$; $M$ acts as identity. ✓
- For $n^* < n \le n^{**}$: $F_n$ contains $h_1$ but not $h_2$; $M(h_1) = c_1 h_1 \in F_n$. ✓
- For $n \le n^*$: $F_n$ contains both; $M(h_1) = c_1 h_1 \in F_{n^*} \subseteq F_n$ and $M(h_2) = ah_1 + c_2 h_2 \in F_{n^*} \subseteq F_n$. ✓

So $M \in P$, $M(H) = H$, $M|_H = \alpha$. By surjectivity, $\alpha$ lifts to $\text{Aut}(G)$.

**Conclusion.** The automorphisms of $H$ that extend to $\text{Aut}(G)$ are exactly the **height-preserving** automorphisms:

$$\boxed{\{\alpha \in \text{Aut}(H) : h_G(\alpha(g)) = h_G(g) \text{ for all } g \in H\}}$$

where $h_G(g) = \max\{n : g \in p^n G\}$ is the $p$-height in $G$. Equivalently, $\alpha$ must preserve the filtration $H \cap p^n G$ for all $n \ge 0$.

Since $\dim_{\mathbb{F}_p} H = 2$, this yields exactly two cases:

- **If all nonzero elements of $H$ have equal height** (i.e., $H$ lies in a single graded piece of the height filtration): every automorphism extends, giving the full group $\text{Aut}(H) \cong \text{GL}(2, \mathbb{F}_p)$.

- **If $H$ contains elements of two distinct heights**: let $L = \{g \in H : h_G(g) \text{ is maximal}\}$, the unique 1-dimensional subspace of maximal-height elements. The extendable automorphisms are exactly those preserving $L$:
$$\{\alpha \in \text{GL}(2,\mathbb{F}_p) : \alpha(L) = L\} \cong \mathbb{F}_p^* \times \mathbb{F}_p^* \ltimes \mathbb{F}_p,$$
i.e., a Borel subgroup of $\text{GL}(2, \mathbb{F}_p)$ (the group of upper-triangular matrices with nonzero diagonal in a basis adapted to $L \subset H$).

